    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That \"remnant of older, more volatile rules\" idea is helpful, but even that feels too structured. When I actually look at the data, it doesn't feel like a \"rule\" is being applied; it feels like something is actually *intervening*.\n\nIt’s as if these tiny, almost microscopic disruptions are actively small-scale thieves of the root's original form. They seem to scuttle into the junctions, hide in the cracks of the stem, and then come out to nibble away at the vowel or change the consonant just enough to mess things up. It gives the whole morphological process this very restless, jittery quality, like there are these tiny, unseen things operating in the dark of the word's internal structure, just waiting for a suffix to arrive so they can start their little work.\n\nIs there any way to model this kind of \"interstitial\" or \"intrusive\" behavior in linguistics? I'm looking for a way to describe how these small, seemingly chaotic elements seem to occupy the gaps and physically alter the shape of the morphemes they inhabit.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
